SNARK synthesis examples
========================

These examples recast several Manna/Waldinger deductive-program-synthesis
examples in SNARK's answer-extraction style.

They are not literal transcripts of the original deductive-tableau proofs.
Instead, each function states the relevant specification/case obligations as
SNARK formulas and asks SNARK to prove the existence of the desired output.  The
answer attached to the refutation is the extracted program term.

Load and run them from a SNARK image:

  (load "examples/synthesis/synthesis-examples.lisp")
  (mw-unification-variable-case)
  (mw-unification-compound-case)
  (mw-square-root-binary-search-step)
  (mw-square-root-binary-search-program)
  (mw-gcd-subtractive-program)
  (mw-quotient-remainder-program)
  (mw-synthesis-examples)

The file also includes executable interpretations of the extracted symbolic
programs:

  (mw-run-unify term1 term2)
  (mw-run-sqrt r eps)
  (mw-run-gcd a b)
  (mw-run-quotient-remainder n d)
  (mw-executable-synthesis-demos)

These functions are intentionally separate from the SNARK proofs.  SNARK
extracts ANSWER-IF terms over abstract symbols such as MW-OCCURS, MW-COMPOSE,
and MW-LE; the MW-RUN-* functions provide ordinary Lisp meanings for those
symbols so the extracted program shapes can be run on concrete inputs.

The square-root examples use abstract predicates such as MW-LE and
MW-LARGE-TOLERANCE.  This keeps the example focused on answer extraction and
the binary-search branching idea rather than on a full first-order development
of real arithmetic.

The GCD and quotient/remainder examples likewise keep arithmetic abstract in
the SNARK proof and provide concrete Common Lisp meanings in the MW-RUN-*
layer.  This makes the distribution show both sides of the development: the
symbolic proof answer and the extra interpretation needed to run it.

New synthesis examples
======================

The file new-synthesis-examples.lisp contains non-Manna/Waldinger examples
intended to exercise the same answer-extraction paradigm on richer program
shapes:

  (new-stable-merge-program)
  (new-stable-partition-program)
  (new-if-normal-form-simplifier-program)
  (new-synthesis-examples)

Their executable interpretations are:

  (new-run-stable-merge xs ys)
  (new-run-stable-partition xs predicate)
  (new-run-if-simplify expr)
  (new-executable-synthesis-demos)

These examples stress recursive calls under constructors, paired outputs,
path-sensitive assumptions, and post-recursion redundancy checks.  That makes
them good next steps beyond the Manna/Waldinger examples without requiring a
full list or IF-expression theory inside SNARK.

Strict MST FOL layer
====================

The file new-mst-fol-synthesis.lisp is the non-probabilistic first-principles
MST pipeline.  It replaces the earlier named obligations with finite
first-order certificates for lists/sets, graph edge membership, paths, cuts,
components, bridge-based acyclicity, weight comparisons, exchange, blue/red
rules, and Boruvka batch safety.

Run:

  (new-mst-fol-synthesis-examples)

The strict layer extracts the program terms for the ordinary MST algorithms
from those lower-level certificates:

  (new-mst-fol-finite-list-membership)
  (new-mst-fol-input-edge-from-finite-list)
  (new-mst-fol-tree-subset-from-finite-lists)
  (new-mst-fol-path-from-finite-list)
  (new-mst-fol-cut-crossing-from-endpoints)
  (new-mst-fol-component-from-path)
  (new-mst-fol-acyclic-from-bridge-cuts)
  (new-mst-fol-weight-comparison)
  (new-mst-fol-lightest-crossing-from-finite-comparisons)
  (new-mst-fol-spanning-tree-from-certificates)
  (new-mst-fol-exchange-from-path-cut-weight)
  (new-mst-fol-blue-rule-from-exchange)
  (new-mst-fol-red-rule-from-cycle)
  (new-mst-fol-batch-safety-from-component-cuts)
  (new-mst-fol-kruskal-from-blue-rule)
  (new-mst-fol-prim-from-blue-rule)
  (new-mst-fol-boruvka-from-batch-safety)

MST FOL prelude
===============

The file new-mst-prelude-synthesis.lisp reifies the first-order objects used by
the MST developments: paths, cycles, cuts, finite edge sets, exchange trees,
contractions, weight comparisons, samples, heap states, matroids, update
streams, and message schedules.

Run:

  (new-mst-prelude-synthesis-examples)

The prelude proves the core schematic lemmas and extracts their witness terms:

  (new-mst-prelude-spanning-tree-definition)
  (new-mst-prelude-mst-definition)
  (new-mst-prelude-crossing-edge-witness)
  (new-mst-prelude-exchange-spanning-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-exchange-weight-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-blue-rule-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-red-rule-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-contract-safe-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-delete-useless-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-boruvka-batch-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-verification-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-replacement-edge-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-second-best-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-f-heavy-filter-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-kkt-filter-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-soft-heap-repair-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-matroid-greedy-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-dynamic-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-ghs-lemma)
  (new-mst-prelude-reverse-delete-algorithm)
  (new-mst-prelude-kruskal-algorithm)
  (new-mst-prelude-prim-algorithm)
  (new-mst-prelude-boruvka-algorithm)
  (new-mst-prelude-filter-kruskal-algorithm)

This prelude is now the broader object vocabulary and advanced-refinement
layer.  For the non-probabilistic correctness spine, use
new-mst-fol-synthesis.lisp as the source of the lower finite FOL derivations.

Hierarchical MST refinements
============================

The file new-mst-synthesis.lisp develops minimum-spanning-tree algorithms as a
chain of logical refinements rather than as one fixed algorithm:

  (new-mst-abstract-specification)
  (new-mst-abstract-greedy-refinement)
  (new-mst-safe-extension-refinement)
  (new-mst-cut-property-refinement)
  (new-mst-kruskal-refinement)
  (new-mst-prim-refinement)
  (new-mst-boruvka-refinement)
  (new-mst-strategy-dispatch-refinement)
  (new-mst-synthesis-examples)

The executable interpretations are:

  (new-run-mst graph :strategy :kruskal)
  (new-run-mst graph :strategy :prim)
  (new-run-mst graph :strategy :boruvka)
  (new-run-mst-demo)

The SNARK layer treats trees, forests, cuts, batches, and strategies as
first-order objects.  The executable layer interprets the extracted leaves over
finite Lisp graphs, showing Kruskal, Prim/Jarnik, and Boruvka as concrete
implementations of the same safe-extension refinement.

Advanced MST refinements
========================

The file new-mst-advanced-synthesis.lisp continues the MST development with
standard refinement and optimization ideas from the MST literature:

  (new-mst-red-rule-refinement)
  (new-mst-reverse-delete-refinement)
  (new-mst-contract-delete-refinement)
  (new-mst-boruvka-phase-refinement)
  (new-mst-kruskal-variants-refinement)
  (new-mst-prim-queue-refinement)
  (new-mst-verification-refinement)
  (new-mst-replacement-edge-refinement)
  (new-mst-second-best-refinement)
  (new-mst-kkt-filter-refinement)
  (new-mst-soft-heap-refinement)
  (new-mst-matroid-greedy-refinement)
  (new-mst-offline-dynamic-refinement)
  (new-mst-ghs-distributed-refinement)
  (new-mst-advanced-synthesis-examples)

The executable interpretations include:

  (new-run-mst-reverse-delete graph)
  (new-run-mst-boruvka-phases graph)
  (new-run-mst-verify graph tree)
  (new-run-mst-replacement-edges graph tree)
  (new-run-mst-second-best tree replacements)
  (new-run-mst-kruskal-variant graph :filter-kruskal)
  (new-run-mst-prim-variant graph :fibonacci-heap)
  (new-run-mst-kkt-filter graph)
  (new-run-mst-advanced-demo)

As in the base MST suite, the SNARK answers are symbolic refinement programs.
The NEW-RUN-MST-* functions instantiate those symbols over finite Lisp graphs
and expose the gap between the extracted proof term and executable data
structures such as union-find, frontier queues, verification paths, and
component contraction.
